  "practice_notes": [
    "The stop's lawfulness is judged by what the officer knew before the stop. Facts learned afterward (smell, admissions, contraband) can't justify it retroactively.",
    "If the stop falls, everything that followed (observations, statements, search results) is potentially fruit — consider which later issues depend on it."
  ],
  "_author_notes": [
    "REVIEW CAREFULLY (legal judgment not yet reviewed): when this issue fires is decided by the 'any' group in trigger.match_condition, keyed to q_stop_reasons and its follow-ups. Those thresholds were drafted by Claude on 2026-10-10 and built at the maintainer's request, but the maintainer has NOT yet agreed with them legally. Check each branch: which stated reasons (alone or together) should suggest this issue, and which should not. The paragraph 'when's (traffic_basis, criminal_basis, general_basis, hunch_insufficient, anonymous_tip) use the same reason groups and need the same review.",
    "Supreme Court citations in this file were added from memory (not checked against the opinions) under the maintainer's 'certain SCOTUS cites only' policy: Delaware v. Prouse, Brendlin v. California, United States v. Cortez, Terry v. Ohio, Brown v. Texas, Florida v. J.L. Unverified; pincites empty.",
    "Gated on vehicle_stop only, per the maintainer ('traffic stop'). Pedestrian stops would need their own gate or issue.",
    "alaska_coleman_standard was supplied by the maintainer (2026-10-08) from one of their filed motions, with its citations and pincites. Alaska only. It applies to all investigative stops (the maintainer, 2026-10-08), so it has no condition on q_stop_type.",
    "Redesigned 2026-10-10 on the 'facts in, issues out' principle: q_stop_basis_problems (a legal-conclusion question) and q_stop_type were replaced by q_stop_reasons (what the officer said the stop was based on) plus factual follow-ups. The thresholds for suggesting this section were drafted by me and built at the maintainer's request, pending legal review (see the REVIEW CAREFULLY note): no reason given; only weak reasons (high-crime area, nervousness, time of day, furtive movements, something else); bad driving with no violation or vehicle problem; a traffic violation or vehicle problem that didn't happen (or not sure); an unidentified, uncorroborated tip; a known vehicle or person where the officer had reason to think it was someone else; a general-only description. Flight and an observed crime don't trigger it alone, and count as real reasons.",
    "strieff_attenuation review note (2026-10-12): drafted by Claude. Strieff (2016) as I recall it: discovering a valid, pre-existing arrest warrant after an unlawful stop attenuated the connection to evidence found in the search incident to the arrest. From memory, unverified. The maintainer chose to show the note in both courts for now.",
    "TO CHECK (the maintainer, 2026-10-12): does Alaska follow Strieff under the Alaska Constitution, or has it rejected or limited it? The strieff_attenuation note prints in state court until this is checked."
  ]
}
